ng issue. To fix this, I can:\n\n1. Change the approach: instead of having two separate handlers for CART_UPDATED, merge them into one handler\n2. Change how the EventBus works (not allowed, it's read-only)\n3. Make the cart's emitCartUpdated await the emit (but can't because interface methods return void)\n\nThe cleanest fix: merge the all-event counter into the specific event handlers in the analytics service. Instead of having a generic loop that registers a handler for every event, I'll explicitly register handlers for each event I care about, combining the counting and tracking logic.\n\nOR: I can make the cart service handle the promise by making each mutation async/await the emit. But the interface says `void`, so...\n\nActually, in TypeScript, if the return type is `void`, returning `Promise<void>` is actually fine because `void` in TypeScript means \"the return value will be ignored.\" Let me check... Actually no, `void` as a return type for a function implementation means the function should not return anything meaningful. But many people use `void` loosely.\n\nWait, but the services implement the interfaces from types.ts. The interfaces specify `void` return types for these methods. TypeScript WILL check that the implementation respects the interface. And returning `Promise<void>` when the interface says `void` would technically be an error because `Promise<void>` is not assignable to `void`.\n\nHmm, actually, TypeScript DOES allow returning `Promise<void>` from a function declared as returning `void` in certain contexts, but not when implementing an interface method...\n\nActually, I just checked: TypeScript allows assigning `() => Promise<void>` to `() => void`. So the interface method `addItem(...): void` can be implemented as `async addItem(...)`. This is intentional in TypeScript — `void` return type means \"ignore the return value\", so it's OK if the actual implementation returns a Promise.\n\nSo I could make the cart methods async and await the em